"""Which of WebKit's shipping JavaScript a browser run executed, from the Inspector protocol.

generate-javascript-coverage measures the 102 builtins inside JavaScriptCore by driving JSC's
control-flow profiler from jsc. That cannot reach the JavaScript that only runs inside a browser --
199,850 lines of Web Inspector front end, WebCore's injected scripts, and the Inspector builtins
that make up 1,049 of the JSC tool's own 2,737 lines. This drives the identical profiler through
the Web Inspector protocol instead, so it measures whatever a real page and a real front end run.

    Tools/Scripts/generate-inspector-js-coverage --target frontend
    Tools/Scripts/generate-inspector-js-coverage --page file:///path/to/page.html
    Tools/Scripts/generate-inspector-js-coverage --target frontend --json /tmp/inspector.json
    Tools/Scripts/generate-inspector-js-coverage --log /tmp/saved-run.log     # re-report only

--target frontend is the interesting one: it opens the real Main.html as the driver page's
inspector, so that the front end boots with an InspectorFrontendHost, and then opens the protocol
test stub as the *front end's* inspector. The inspector inspecting the inspector, in one process,
one VM, and therefore one ControlFlowProfiler.

The headline is the list of functions nothing called, by file, function and line, and the
least-covered files. Every number is a lower bound and printed with a >=: one page load is a
subset of what the product does, in exactly the way Tools/CodeCoverage/README.md describes.

HOW, in one paragraph. A generated HTML page is run under WebKitTestRunner. It uses
Internals::openDummyInspectorFrontend and WebInspectorUI's TestStub.html -- the same machinery
LayoutTests/inspector/* runs on -- to get a protocol channel, sends Runtime.enableControlFlowProfiler
and Debugger.enable, reloads the target because enabling the profiler deletes all compiled code,
waits for Debugger.scriptParsed to go quiet, and then asks Runtime.getBasicBlocks for every
sourceID the backend named. See webkitpy/coverage_inspector.py for what getBasicBlocks actually
returns, which is two different kinds of record, and for the one that has to be filtered out
before any line is attributed.

IT WRITES NO PROFILES INTO THE SHARED DIRECTORY. Every binary in a coverage build has
/private/tmp/WebKitCoverage/<Product>_%8m%c.profraw baked in, and that path is machine-global.
Both LLVM_PROFILE_FILE and __XPC_LLVM_PROFILE_FILE are always set, the second because the
Network, GPU and WebContent processes are XPC services that see only the prefixed copies -- with
only the first set, one run left 20 files and 409 MB of .profraw in the shared
directory.
"""

import json
import logging
import optparse
import os
import shutil
import sys
import tempfile

from webkitpy.common.system.filesystem import FileSystem
from webkitpy.common.webkit_finder import WebKitFinder
from webkitpy.coverage_inspector import (
    COVERAGE_BEGIN, TARGET_FRONTEND, TARGET_PAGE, TARGETS, InspectorCoverageReport, PayloadError,
    WebKitTestRunnerDriver, default_workload_source, driver_source, extract_payload, file_url,
    find_inspector_resource, find_test_runner, format_report, scripts_from_payload)
from webkitpy.coverage_scope import CoverageScope

_log = logging.getLogger('generate-inspector-js-coverage')

# Same list and same order as generate-javascript-coverage, for the same reason: the coverage tree
# is what Tools/CodeCoverage's recipes produce, and a plain Release build works just as well.
_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES = (
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'cmake-mac', 'Coverage'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild-Coverage', 'Release'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'Release'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'cmake-mac', 'Release'),
    os.path.join('WebKitBuild', 'Debug'),
)

_EPILOG = """
WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN

Functions is a LOWER BOUND on both halves. Runtime.getBasicBlocks reports a text range for every
function a compiled CodeBlock declared, whether or not it was ever called, so a function nothing
called is visible and named -- but a function inside a function that was never compiled is not
there at all, and neither is one whose range does not start on something this tool recognises as a
function head. What the number says is: of the functions the profiler saw, this many ran.

Lines is per basic block, over the code lines of each source: non-blank, non-comment,
non-punctuation-only, which is the identical denominator generate-javascript-coverage uses and the
rule Tools/CodeCoverage/README.md states for the native pipeline. A line is covered when a range
that overlaps a non-whitespace character of it executed. Ranges that enclose other ranges are
discarded first: a function's whole-text range reads as executed the moment the function is
entered once, and crediting it would report every function that ran at all as fully covered.

A source with no basic blocks is not a source with no coverage. Reloading the target is what makes
the profiler see it, and a script the reload did not reach -- a lazily loaded module, a worker --
has no record either way and is counted under "sources with basic blocks" rather than at 0%.

WHAT IS NOT MEASURABLE THIS WAY

WebCore's builtins. They are compiled as one SourceProvider per builtin function with no URL, and
Debugger::attach skips builtin functions on purpose (Debugger.cpp:196,
`!function->isHostOrBuiltinFunction()`), so no scriptParsed event ever names them and
Debugger.getScriptSource cannot be asked about a sourceID the debugger has not registered. Their
blocks are in the profiler; there is no protocol call that enumerates them. See
Tools/CodeCoverage/JavaScriptCoverage.md for the measurement that shows why scanning sourceIDs by
hand does not work either.

WebCore's injected media controls. They arrive as one 161 KB script with no URL because
make-js-file-arrays.py runs jsmin over the concatenation of the 87 authored files: 9,892 source
lines become 2,042, and there is no source map. The script is measured and reported under its
sourceURL directive; it cannot be split back into files without building with --no-minify.
"""


class _PreformattedEpilog(optparse.IndentedHelpFormatter):
    def format_epilog(self, epilog):
        return epilog or ''


def parse_args(args):
    parser = optparse.OptionParser(usage='%prog [options]', epilog=_EPILOG,
                                   formatter=_PreformattedEpilog())
    parser.add_option('--target', type='choice', choices=list(TARGETS), default=TARGET_PAGE,
                      help='What to measure: "page" for the JavaScript a page runs, "frontend" '
                           'for the Web Inspector front end itself (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--page', metavar='URL',
                      help='The page to load and measure with --target page. Defaults to a '
                           'generated workload that exercises WebCore\'s stream builtins and the '
                           'injected media controls. Ignored with --target frontend')
    parser.add_option('--build-directory',
                      help='Build directory holding WebKitTestRunner and WebInspectorUI. '
                           'Defaults to the first of {} that has both'.format(
                               ', '.join(_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES)))
    parser.add_option('--driver', help='Path to WebKitTestRunner, if not the build directory\'s')
    parser.add_option('--settle-ms', type='int', default=3000, metavar='MS',
                      help='How long to let the front end boot before inspecting it, with '
                           '--target frontend (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--quiet-polls', type='int', default=6, metavar='N',
                      help='Consecutive 500ms polls with no new Debugger.scriptParsed event that '
                           'end the measurement (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--fetch-sources', type='choice',
                      choices=['none', 'unresolved', 'all'], default='unresolved',
                      help='Which sources to pull back with Debugger.getScriptSource: "none", '
                           '"unresolved" for the ones whose URL is not a file: URL, or "all" '
                           '(default: %default). "all" is exact and slow -- it moves megabytes '
                           'through the log -- and is only needed when the checkout has moved on '
                           'from the build')
    parser.add_option('--timeout', type='int', default=600, metavar='SECONDS',
                      help='Kill WebKitTestRunner after this long (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--log', metavar='PATH',
                      help='Report on an already-captured WebKitTestRunner log instead of running '
                           'anything')
    parser.add_option('--save-log', metavar='PATH',
                      help='Write the run\'s stderr here, so it can be re-reported with --log')
    parser.add_option('--save-driver', metavar='DIR',
                      help='Keep the generated driver and workload pages in DIR instead of a '
                           'temporary directory, so they can be run by hand')
    parser.add_option('--profile-destination', metavar='PATH', default=os.devnull,
                      help='Where an instrumented build should write .profraw files (default: '
                           '%default). Never the baked-in machine-global path')
    parser.add_option('--limit', type='int', default=40, metavar='N',
                      help='How many never-executed functions and how many files to list '
                           '(default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('--json', metavar='PATH', help='Also write the report as JSON')
    parser.add_option('-v', '--verbose', action='store_true', default=False,
                      help='Verbose logging')
    options, remaining = parser.parse_args(args)
    if remaining:
        parser.error('Unexpected arguments: {}. The page to measure is --page.'.format(
            ' '.join(remaining)))
    if options.quiet_polls < 1:
        parser.error('--quiet-polls must be at least 1')
    return options


def resolve_build_directory(options, checkout_root):
    """(build directory, WebKitTestRunner, TestStub.html, Main.html).

    All four have to come from one build: the stub and Main.html are loaded by absolute file URL
    and the driver is the process that loads them, so a mismatch would be a version-mismatch
    crash rather than a wrong number.
    """
    candidates = [options.build_directory] if options.build_directory else [
        os.path.join(checkout_root, relative) for relative in _BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES]
    for candidate in candidates:
        driver = options.driver or find_test_runner(candidate)
        stub = find_inspector_resource(candidate, 'TestStub.html')
        main = find_inspector_resource(candidate, 'Main.html')
        if driver and stub:
            return candidate, driver, stub, main
    return None, options.driver, None, None


def write_driver(directory, options, stub_path, main_path):
    """(driver page path, page URL) for this run."""
    if options.target == TARGET_FRONTEND:
        page_url = 'about:blank'
    elif options.page:
        page_url = options.page
    else:
        workload_path = os.path.join(directory, 'webkit-inspector-coverage-workload.html')
        with open(workload_path, 'w') as handle:
            handle.write(default_workload_source())
        page_url = file_url(workload_path)

    driver_path = os.path.join(directory, 'webkit-inspector-coverage-driver.html')
    with open(driver_path, 'w') as handle:
        handle.write(driver_source(
            target=options.target, page=page_url,
            frontend_url=file_url(main_path) if main_path else None,
            stub_url=file_url(stub_path), settle_ms=options.settle_ms,
            quiet_polls=options.quiet_polls, fetch_sources=options.fetch_sources))
    return driver_path, page_url


def main(args):
    options = parse_args(args)
    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG if options.verbose else logging.INFO,
                        format='%(message)s')

    checkout_root = WebKitFinder(FileSystem()).webkit_base()

    if options.log:
        with open(options.log, 'r', encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as handle:
            log_text = handle.read()
        page_url = None
        driver_path = None
    else:
        build_directory, driver_path, stub_path, main_path = resolve_build_directory(
            options, checkout_root)
        if not driver_path or not stub_path:
            _log.error('Found no WebKitTestRunner and WebInspectorUI/TestStub.html together. '
                       'Looked in %s. Pass --build-directory, or --log to report on an '
                       'already-captured run.', ', '.join(_BUILD_DIRECTORY_CANDIDATES))
            return 1
        if options.target == TARGET_FRONTEND and not main_path:
            _log.error('--target frontend needs the build\'s WebInspectorUI Main.html and %s does '
                       'not have one', build_directory)
            return 1

        temporary = options.save_driver or tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='webkit-inspector-coverage-')
        os.makedirs(temporary, exist_ok=True)
        try:
            driver_page, page_url = write_driver(temporary, options, stub_path, main_path)
            driver = WebKitTestRunnerDriver(
                driver_path, build_directory,
                profile_destination=options.profile_destination)
            _log.info('Running %s over %s, target %s',
                      os.path.relpath(driver_path, checkout_root),
                      os.path.basename(driver_page), options.target)
            _log.debug('%s', ' '.join(driver.command(driver_page)))
            returncode, stdout, log_text = driver.run(driver_page, timeout=options.timeout)
        finally:
            if not options.save_driver:
                shutil.rmtree(temporary, ignore_errors=True)
        if returncode is None:
            _log.error('WebKitTestRunner did not finish within %ds', options.timeout)
        summary = next((line for line in stdout.splitlines()
                        if line.startswith(('scripts=', 'error', 'could not', 'the front end'))),
                       None)
        if summary:
            _log.info('  %s', summary)
        if options.save_log:
            with open(options.save_log, 'w') as handle:
                handle.write(log_text)
            _log.info('Wrote %s', options.save_log)

    try:
        payload = extract_payload(log_text)
    except PayloadError as failure:
        _log.error('%s', failure)
        _log.error('The log is %d bytes. Re-run with --save-log and read it: the driver reports '
                   'its own failures on the same stream, before %s.', len(log_text),
                   COVERAGE_BEGIN)
        return 1

    coverages = scripts_from_payload(payload, checkout_root)
    scope = CoverageScope.selective(
        [payload.get('target') or options.target, payload.get('page') or page_url or '(log)'],
        tests_run=1, suite_name='inspector-protocol')
    report = InspectorCoverageReport(
        coverages, target=payload.get('target') or options.target,
        page=payload.get('page') or page_url, driver=driver_path, scope=scope)

    for line in format_report(report, limit=options.limit):
        print(line)
    for line in scope.banner_lines():
        print(line)

    if options.json:
        with open(options.json, 'w') as handle:
            json.dump(report.to_json(), handle, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
            handle.write('\n')
        _log.info('Wrote %s', options.json)

    if not report.files:
        _log.error('No source produced usable coverage, so every number above is zero for want '
                   'of data rather than for want of coverage')
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
